-- **How the front end maps the new syntax.** The parser makes a new node for a clause block in the syntax tree, `ast::ExprKind::Clauses`, and a match is a `|>` whose right side is such a node. The parser does not rewrite it. Resolve, not the parser, lowers it onto the existing resolved tree:
-  - A block with one clause, no guard, and no choices becomes the existing nodes of a binding or a parameter, so that the checker and the lowering do not change for it. The match `e |> { p -> body }` becomes the block `{ p = e  body }`, so `e` gets the expected type of the binding, as §4.8 says, and a lone block `{ p -> body }` becomes the function `(p) { body }`, with the label of `p` from `label_of`. These nodes are already fitted, already report to `Assert`, and already have the catch-all that calls `a.fail`. This is the one WAT difference that the plan allows for A1.
-  - Spans of the block `{ p = e  body }`: the `Stmt::Bind` runs from the start of `e` to the end of `p`, and does not cover `body`, so that `query.rs` finds `e` in it and offers the names of `p` in `body`. Its stop position is the start of `p` (§6.12). The span cannot start at `p` instead, because `p` comes after `e` in the source, so the span would run backwards, and `query.rs` would no longer find `e` in it. Today the lowering of a local binding takes the stop position from the start of the binding: `binding()` in `crates/ilang-wasm/src/lower.rs` calls `bind_checked(…, b.span.start)`. So this needs a one-line change there, to `b.pattern.span.start`. For a binding written in the source, the two positions are the same, so the change gives no different output today. Without it, `get = (opt) { opt |> { Some(x) -> x } }` called with `None` stops with the position of `opt`, not of `Some(x)`, against §6.12 and `tests/spec/9/parameter_fails.i`. The plan does not let A1 change a line of `lower.rs`, so the coordinator adds this line to the files of A1 and to the exceptions of the plan (see "Edits outside the ranges of A1" below).
