-  - When the binding that owned it has a pattern that is not one name, as in `(wrap, unwrap) = (…, …)`: inside another binding, declare the variable on the nearest binding of one name around it, and report it, because the local binding is then less general. At the top level, split the binding into one binding of one name for each part, when its value is a tuple with one part for each name, and declare the variables on each. When it cannot be split, report it, and leave it for the user to change.
-- **Matches in operator expressions.** The formatter's migration puts in a group (§6.9) every old `case` that is an operand of an operator of level 7 or 8 or of `-` or `-.`, or that has `.f`, `.k`, or a call after it. So `1 + case n { … }` becomes `1 + { n |> { … } }`, and `case x { _ -> 1 }.y` becomes `{ x |> { _ -> 1 } }.y`. It also puts the subject `S` of an old `case S { … }` in a group when the top operator of `S` is at level 1 to 5 (`||`, `&&`, `==`, `!=`, a comparison, or `<>`), because `|>` is stronger than these (§6.9): `case S { … }` becomes `{ S } |> { … }`. Without the group, `case times > 1 { … }` in the example `retry` of §9.1 would become `times > (1 |> { … })`, and `case a <> b { … }` would become `a <> (b |> { … })`, which may be correct code with another meaning. This is needed in §9.1, in `std/src/float.i`, and in the tests with `case got == want { … }`. It also puts each clause or statement whose pattern starts with `(`, `-`, or `-.` on a new line (§1.7).
+  - When the owner pattern is not one name, the migration never declares its variable on an outer binding: that would reduce generality. It splits an all-direct tuple value into separate bindings only when the names are distinct and free of cross-dependencies; otherwise it refuses the file as `tyvar-owner`. A variable with no owner is renamed and declared nowhere, with a `hand` entry `tyvar-unowned` for E0222 → E0202. Mixed reserved heads whose rename would create a valid parameter list are refused too (A1 R4 and R10).
+- **Matches in operator expressions.** The formatter's migration puts in a group (§6.9) every old `case` that is an operand of an operator of level 7 or 8 or of `-` or `-.`, or that has `.f`, `.k`, or a call after it. So `1 + case n { … }` becomes `1 + { n |> { … } }`, and `case x { _ -> 1 }.y` becomes `{ x |> { _ -> 1 } }.y`. It also puts the subject `S` of an old `case S { … }` in a group when the top operator of `S` is at level 1 to 5 (`||`, `&&`, `==`, `!=`, a comparison, or `<>`), because `|>` is stronger than these (§6.9): `case S { … }` becomes `{ S } |> { … }`. Without the group, `case times > 1 { … }` in the example `retry` of §9.1 would become `times > (1 |> { … })`, and `case a <> b { … }` would become `a <> (b |> { … })`, which may be correct code with another meaning. This is needed in §9.1, in `std/src/float.i`, and in the tests with `case got == want { … }`. For an old case on the left of any binary operator, it also groups the whole match to preserve stop-site count (A1 R2c). This rule applies only to old cases: a new one-clause match may give its subject an expected type. It puts each clause or statement whose output starts with `(`, `-`, `-.`, or `{` on a new line (§1.7 and A1 R8); splice mode refuses the insertion when the file has CRs.
 - **Guards after function types.** When a clause has a guard and its pattern ends in a type whose last part is a function type, the migration puts that function type in `{ }` before it writes `?` (§7.1). So `f: (Int) -> Int if ok -> 1` becomes `f: { (Int) -> Int } ? ok -> 1`, and `x: Int | (Int) -> Int if g -> …` becomes `x: Int | { (Int) -> Int } ? g -> …`. Without this, the `?` would be read as the error part of the function type, and with `! N` in the type, a `?` after it would be a compile error.
-- **First clauses that give a function.** When the first clause of an old `case` has a pattern `n1 = … = nk = ( … )`, one or more names each followed by `=` and then a tuple pattern, as in §6.1 test 2, and the text after its `->` reads as the `->` part of a function followed by `{` on the same line (§6.1 test 2), the migration puts the whole clause expression in a group: `p = (a, b) -> () { log(p) }` becomes `p = (a, b) -> { () { log(p) } }`, and `p = (a, b) -> () { a }()` becomes `p = (a, b) -> { () { a }() }`. This holds for every clause expression that starts with a function, also when a call, an operator, or `|>` comes after the function. When that function has a `->` part, a group alone is read as a clause block (§6.1), so the migration also binds the function to a name in the group, and puts the name in its place: `p = (a, b) -> (x) -> Int { x + a }` becomes `p = (a, b) -> { g = (x) -> Int { x + a }  g }`, and `p = (a, b) -> (x) -> Int { x + a }(1)` becomes `p = (a, b) -> { g = (x) -> Int { x + a }  g(1) }`. The name is the first of `g`, `g2`, `g3`, … that is not used in the clause. So `a = b = (x, y) -> () { x }` becomes `a = b = (x, y) -> { () { x } }`.
-- **Functions with a `->` part at the start of a `{`.** When the first statement of a `{` that begins an expression in the migrated code starts with a function that has a `->` part and only patterns as parameters, the `{` would be read as a clause block (§6.1). This holds for old blocks and groups, for the groups that the migration adds, and for the subject of an old `case` that becomes the first statement of such a `{`. The migration binds the function to a name first, as a new statement before it, and puts the name in its place: `r = { (x) -> Int { x + n }(1) }` becomes `r = { g = (x) -> Int { x + n }  g(1) }`, and `{ case (x) -> Int { x }(1) { _ -> 0 } }` becomes `{ g = (x) -> Int { x }  g(1) |> { _ -> 0 } }`. The name is chosen as in the note above, from the names not used in that `{`. The body of a function is not such a `{` and does not change.
-  - An old block such as `r = { (x) -> Int { x + n }(1) }` is correct old code, but the parser that accepts both syntaxes reads it by the tests of §6.1, as a clause block with the pattern `(x)` and the expression `Int`, and then fails on `{ x + n }(1)`. There is no keyword in it to show that it is old. So the migration does not read old files with that parser. It reads them with a mode of A1's parser that takes only the old forms, in which a `{` that begins an expression is always a block, as in the old syntax, and no new form is read. The formatter then writes the new syntax with the rewrites of these notes. `i fmt` on a file in the new syntax uses the parser for both, as usual, and needs no such rewrite, because there such a `{` is already a clause block. A1-D decides how `i fmt` chooses the mode for a file, for example by a flag of the migration.
+- **First clauses that give a function.** For one first pattern and no guard, a chain `n1 = … = nk = ( … )` followed by a function expression may be read as a function binding (§6.1 test 2). The migration groups that expression when its function has no `->` part. With a `->` part, a group alone is a clause block, and an untyped `g = F` would lose the expected type of the clause expression. The file is therefore refused as `fn-expected-type`; the hand edit writes the expected type on `g`. Extraction is safe only where the function has no expected type, as A1 R5 and R6 specify. Choices or a guard prevent the immediate-arrow ambiguity, so this rule does not group them.
